  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 320 AM EDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nA ridge remains in place over the southeastern United States on \nFriday into the weekend. Little change in the temperature trends are \nlikely because of this. Higher than average temperatures will remain \nin place - with temperatures around 8-13 degrees higher than \nclimatology through Sunday. Daytime highs will be in the upper 80s \nto upper 90s, with heat indices from around 90 to 106F.\n\nSufficient moisture and instability will be in place to sustain \ndiurnal convection. Moisture levels, and therefore precip chances, \nwill be greater over central Georgia than northern Georgia and the \nAtlanta metro, where PoPs will be around 20% or less. The greatest \nrisk for thunderstorms will be on Sunday, with additional moisture \nand instability, associated with the arrival of a frontal \nboundary.\n\nSlightly cooler air is expected behind the frontal boundary on \nMonday, as a ridge of high pressure centered over Hudson Bay sinks \nsouthward. Still, daytime highs will remain above climatology by \naround 5 to 10 degrees, with highs in the low 80s to mid 90s across \nnorthern and central Georgia. Though PoPs decrease somewhat on \nMonday, and moreso on Tuesday, there remains the potential for \ndiurnal convection, with isolated to scattered afternoon/evening \nthunderstorms.",
